---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocFigure from '../../../../components/DocFigure.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Ingest Footage — MHA Manager"
  description="Bring raw capture footage into your project as Capture Data assets and queue it — manually or automatically."
  currentSlug="ingest"
>

  <div class="doc-header">
    <h1 class="doc-title">Ingest Footage</h1>
    <p class="doc-lead">
      Every footage job runs on a <code>UFootageCaptureData</code> asset. Getting your raw footage
      into the project as that asset is <em>ingest</em>, and Epic's Live Link Hub — which bundles
      Capture Manager — is the engine that does it. You can drive it from Live Link Hub's own
      window, or from the plugin's <strong>Add Jobs...</strong> dialog without leaving the editor.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Live Link Hub</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      <strong>Live Link Hub</strong> is Epic's first-party capture application and the recommended
      input for MHA Manager. It bundles Capture Manager: connect your capture source (Live Link Face
      on iPhone, a stereo head-mounted camera, or an existing take archive), import the takes, and
      ingest them into your Unreal project. Each take lands in the Content Browser as a
      <code>UFootageCaptureData</code> asset — exactly what every job runs on — with none of the
      manual asset assembly you would otherwise face.
    </p>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/llh-capture-manager.webp"
      alt="Live Link Hub — Capture Manager view with takes selected, ingesting into the Unreal project"
      caption="Live Link Hub's Capture Manager ingests takes straight into the project as Capture Data"
    />
    <p class="section-desc">
      Because Live Link Hub writes finished Capture Data directly into the project, nothing has to be
      assembled by hand afterwards. Turn on <strong>Auto-Queue Ingested Takes</strong> (see below) and
      every take Live Link Hub delivers is classified and queued automatically — capture in one
      window, watch jobs appear in the other.
    </p>
    <DocFigure
      src="/assets/docs/mha/llh-autoqueue-result.webp"
      alt="Content Browser with newly ingested Capture Data assets beside the MHA Manager panel showing matching auto-queued job rows"
      caption="With Auto-Queue on, takes ingested by Live Link Hub arrive as queued jobs on their own"
    />
    <div class="callout callout-note">
      Live Link Hub is a separate Epic download and depends on the Live Link / Virtual Production
      plugins. See Epic's Live Link Hub and MetaHuman Animator capture documentation for device
      setup and recording — that side of the workflow is Epic's, not this plugin's.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Ingest from the Add Jobs dialog</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/add-jobs"><strong>Add Jobs...</strong></a> in the MHA
      Manager panel covers all three ways work enters the queue, including raw footage that has not
      been ingested yet:
    </p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="opt-row header">
        <span>Source</span>
        <span>What it queues</span>
      </div>
      <div class="opt-row">
        <span><strong>External Footage (Live Link Hub)</strong></span>
        <span>Pick a raw take folder on disk and launch Live Link Hub to ingest it into the project. Requires the <code>CaptureManagerEditor</code> plugin (UE 5.7+).</span>
      </div>
      <div class="opt-row">
        <span><strong>Project Assets</strong></span>
        <span>One job per Footage Capture Data asset already in the project — takes ingested earlier, or by someone else on the team.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="opt-row">
        <span><strong>Audio</strong></span>
        <span>Audio-driven jobs from project SoundWaves, a folder of WAVs on disk, or a Wwise container over WAAPI. No footage and no identity needed. Requires UE 5.8.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Auto-queue on ingest</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      As takes arrive from Live Link Hub / Capture Manager, the <strong>Ingest</strong> section
      of <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/global-settings">Global Settings</a> can watch the
      project and queue them the moment they appear in the Content Browser:
    </p>
    <div class="field-list">
      <div class="opt-row header">
        <span>Setting</span>
        <span>What it does</span>
      </div>
      <div class="opt-row">
        <span><strong>Auto-Queue Ingested Takes</strong></span>
        <span>Watches the project for newly created Footage Capture Data assets and adds each to the queue automatically. Takes already in the queue are not re-added.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="opt-row">
        <span><strong>Auto-Start Batch on Ingest</strong></span>
        <span>Starts the batch when auto-queued takes arrive and no batch is running. Takes arriving mid-batch are queued and started on the next idle trigger.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="opt-row">
        <span><strong>Watch Folder (optional)</strong></span>
        <span>A content-path prefix to limit the watch, e.g. <code>/Game/CaptureManager/Ingest</code>. Empty watches everywhere under <code>/Game</code>.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">What happens to an ingested take</h2>
    <p class="section-desc">
      However a take enters the queue, it is classified automatically and readied to process:
    </p>
    <ul class="bullet-list">
      <li>Its <a href="/marketplace/mha-manager/docs/job-types">job type</a> is derived from the take — path-name tokens drive <code>grid</code>/<code>calib</code> -&gt; Stereo Calibration and <code>identity</code>/<code>dna</code> -&gt; Identity Create, while mono / stereo / audio come from the asset itself.</li>
      <li>Mono takes get a first-frame framing probe that auto-enables body tracking when the whole body is in shot.</li>
      <li>Dependent assets (calibration, identity) are auto-discovered where the folder layout allows, so a stereo take lands ready or clearly marked for what it still needs.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      Keep <code>grid</code>, <code>calib</code>, <code>identity</code>, and <code>dna</code> out of
      folder and take names that are <em>not</em> those take types — those tokens drive
      classification, and a misnamed performance take will be queued as the wrong job.
    </div>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .doc-header { margin-bottom: 32px; }

  .doc-title {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .doc-lead {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 620px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .doc-lead code,
  .section-desc code,
  .opt-row code,
  .bullet-list code,
  .callout code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .doc-section { margin-bottom: 44px; }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .subsection-title {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin: 32px 0 12px;
  }

  .section-desc {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .section-desc strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .bullet-list {
    padding-left: 20px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
  }

  .bullet-list li {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding-left: 4px;
  }

  .bullet-list li strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .field-list {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .opt-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 2.4fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 11px 16px;
    font-size: 0.825rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    align-items: start;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .opt-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .opt-row.header {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .opt-row strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 500; }

  .callout {
    padding: 12px 16px;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: 0.825rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }

  .callout code { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg-surface) 60%, transparent); }
  .callout strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  .callout em { color: var(--text); font-style: italic; }

  .callout-note {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 8%, transparent);
  }

  .callout-tip {
    border-left: 3px solid #f0a500;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, #f0a500 8%, transparent);
  }

  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .opt-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
